"CONSUMER CONTRACTS\nreduces doubt. Thus, if the meaning of a term is reasonably susceptible to more than one\ninterpretation; the meaning of a term is uncertain, ambiguous, or otherwise lacks clarity; and other\nfactors relevant to the construction of the meaning of a term are not decisive, there is substantial\nreason for preferring a reasonable meaning attached by the party with less reason to know or\nunderstand the other side's meaning-typically the consumer. See Restatement of the Law Second,\nContracts §§ 201 and 206.\nIllustration:\n1. A life-insurance contract contains an exclusion for a person \"engaged in\naviation.\" In a claim by a consumer who was a passenger on a commercial flight that\ncrashed, the court concludes that the term \"engaged in aviation\" is susceptible to two\npossible interpretations-riding an airplane or operating it-and it is therefore ambiguous.\nThe narrower interpretation of the exclusion, which favors the consumer, is preferred.\n4. Interpretation of process statements, adoption directions, and notices against the\nbusiness. In scrutinizing the reasonableness of the process by which standard contract terms are\nadopted, the statements, adoption directions, and notices provided by the business to inform\nconsumers about the consequences of their actions are evaluated. See §§ 2 and 3. Like the\ninterpretation of the substance of the standard contract terms, if such statements, directions, and\nnotices are susceptible to more than one reasonable understanding, an interpretation that favors the\nconsumer is preferred.\nIllustration:\n2. A checkout page on a business's website contains a scroll box titled \"Service\nAgreement\" showing only the first few lines of the standard contract terms. Below the box\nappears a sentence stating, \"You understand that by clicking 'I Accept' you are also\nproviding authorization to the business to obtain your credit report.\" Below that sentence\nappears a click button labeled \"I Accept.\" This notice is susceptible to two interpretations\nregarding the effect of clicking \"I Accept\"-that it only authorizes a credit report, or that\nit also operates as acceptance of the terms of the Service Agreement in the scroll box-and\nit is therefore ambiguous. An interpretation that favors the consumer is preferred.\n90\n@§4\n"